{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed it: `--out=log.txt` now parses to `{\"out\": \"log.txt\"}`.\n\nThe bug was in `parse_flags` in `repo/cli.py`. It never split on the `=`, so it treated the whole `out=log.txt` as the flag name and set its value to `True`. It also had a second problem: if a plain argument came right after an `=` flag, it took that argument as the flag's value.\n\nThe parser now splits on the first `=` only and doesn't look at the next argument. So `--out=a=b.txt` gives `\"a=b.txt\"`, and `--x=` gives an empty string. The `--key value` and bare `--flag` forms work as before.\n\nBoth tests in `tests/visible/test_flags.py` pass, and I checked those edge cases by hand."}]},"session_id":"235a2e7d-307e-4304-a1b2-ed0fe3a6d1ff"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":26411,"duration_api_ms":26411,"is_error":false,"result":"Let me look at the workspace to find the argument parsing code.The `--key=value` form never got split, so `--out=log.txt` was stored as the flag name `\"out=log.txt\"` with the value `True`. I'll split on the first `=`.I fixed it: `--out=log.txt` now parses to `{\"out\": \"log.txt\"}`.\n\nThe bug was in `parse_flags` in `repo/cli.py`. It never split on the `=`, so it treated the whole `out=log.txt` as the flag name and set its value to `True`. It also had a second problem: if a plain argument came right after an `=` flag, it took that argument as the flag's value.\n\nThe parser now splits on the first `=` only and doesn't look at the next argument. So `--out=a=b.txt` gives `\"a=b.txt\"`, and `--x=` gives an empty string. The `--key value` and bare `--flag` forms work as before.\n\nBoth tests in `tests/visible/test_flags.py` pass, and I checked those edge cases by hand.","session_id":"235a2e7d-307e-4304-a1b2-ed0fe3a6d1ff","request_id":"fb54c8a8-7e0e-4421-b85e-436ec80cc77b","usage":{"inputTokens":10,"outputTokens":1465,"cacheReadTokens":88189,"cacheWriteTokens":19072}}
